KG42.register("nodes-49c3caf55b3b6b0c5462c77d",{"id":"COMP-464efdadf92efbd8","code":"OMC018","label":"Coordinator for selecting system-service suppliers","definition":"A coordinating entity that, in response to power system operation service demand, organizes community service supply and holds the decision-making authority to select supply resources.","layer":"L4","template_code":"SRP","concept_id":"coordination.local_operator","concept_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"A coordinating entity that, in response to power system operation service demand, organizes community service supply and holds the decision-making authority to select supply resources.","object":"The organizational responsibilities and supply selection authority among the system service demand side, community suppliers, and the coordinating entity.","inputs":"System operation service demand and community candidate service supply information.","operation":"Based on the service demand, the coordinating entity decides which community supplies to adopt within the supply conditions and participant authority specified by the source.","outputs":"The selection decision for community service supply and the attribution of coordination responsibility.","boundary":"Limited to organizational responsibilities and resource selection authority within L4 and SRP. Whether the demand side and the coordinator are undertaken by the same entity, direct control rights, baseline approval, and procurement procedures are all retained as membership conditions; clearing algorithms, price formation, or payment formulas are not merged, nor is the coordinator required to have unrestricted unilateral control."},"exclusions":[["SESP also serves as the local platform provider and community aggregator, and does not possess grid state information when receiving DSO requests.","The request is the minimum active energy change relative to the forecast baseline; positive and negative values indicate upward and downward regulation, respectively; it is not equated with another member's kW demand value.","SESP generates a forecast of flexible assets, which must be approved by the DSO before it can be used as a baseline.","In the yellow-light state, DSO demand has the highest priority, and SESP may override household decisions; the source text attributes direct control under the red light to the DSO, and this cannot be expanded into a general authority of SESP.","The SESP market subsystem is responsible for transaction, settlement, billing, and accounting management; these are organizational responsibilities, not common payment rules."],["The utility organizes procurement according to future system operation needs and designates Type-1 AS or Type-2 AS.","Publish D^{AS}={D_t^{AS}|t∈T} and p^{AS}={p_t^{AS}|t∈T}, with units of kW and £/kW/h, respectively; the price is fixed when the customer declares.","Within the Agreement Period, a declaration collection window can be set, and a procurement decision is made after collection.","Procurement quotas are in principle equally divided, and any surplus beyond customer declarations is reallocated; the given source context also retains the customer's authority, when the quota is insufficient, to choose the final quantity between zero and the allocated quota.","The utility-side method for deriving demand and prices is outside the scope of the paper, and common optimization or pricing rules cannot be fabricated."]],"members":[{"id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The DSO's requested flexibility supplies the input for determining local resource allocations over the operation day. Scope: SESP scheduling of local flexible resources for the operation day. The requested upward active-energy variation imposes a minimum on the net upward regulation allocated relative to the forecasted baseline; contributions opposing the request remain included in the constraint. Scope: Upward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem. Condition: During upward-regulation periods with a positive DSO request. The requested downward active-energy variation imposes a minimum on the magnitude of net downward regulation allocated relative to the forecasted baseline; contributions opposing the request remain included in the constraint. Scope: Downward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem. Condition: During downward-regulation periods with a negative DSO request. The DSO sends its flexibility needs before the SESP executes the daily local allocation algorithm. Scope: Daily allocation sequence described for the case study. The SESP, acting as the local market coordinator, determines which flexible resources are allocated to meet the DSO request. Scope: SESP-operated local flexibility market serving DSO power-quality requirements. Local allocation minimizes the SESP's operating cost of meeting the DSO request over the following periods, using flexibility costs predefined in SESP-community member contracts. Scope: Objective of the local flexibility allocation problem. Local flexibility allocation is restricted to providing flexibility to the DSO. Scope: Service scope of the presented local flexibility market. DSO needs receive maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads; prosumer or aggregator priorities are not considered. Scope: Local allocation priorities under the Traffic Light Concept used in the formulation. Condition: During yellow light situations. Allocation must respect the contracted times during which the offered flexibility can be used. Scope: Availability restrictions on contracted flexibility used in local allocation. Condition: When allocating resources whose flexibility contracts specify permitted usage times. Allocated generation curtailment is nonnegative and cannot exceed the forecasted generation. Scope: Reducible-generator feasibility constraints in local allocation. Condition: For reducible generators in each scheduling period. Allocation permits only switching these generators on or off; intermediate production setpoints are unavailable. Scope: Disconnectable-generator feasibility restrictions in local allocation. Condition: For disconnectable generators. Battery allocations must respect per-period charging and discharging limits and maximum storage capacity, with charging and discharging efficiencies included in state-of-charge accounting. Scope: Battery feasibility constraints in local allocation. Condition: When allocating battery charging or discharging. The allocation schedule must either maintain the disconnection or reconnect the load in the current period. Scope: Continuity of curtailable disconnectable load schedules in local allocation. Condition: After a load was disconnected or remained disconnected in the preceding period. Allocation schedules must respect the maximum number of disconnection orders per day, the minimum resting time between disconnections and the maximum disconnection duration, as applicable. Scope: Configured operating bounds for curtailable disconnectable loads in local allocation. Condition: When the corresponding disconnection-count, resting-time or duration bounds are specified. Curtailable disconnectable loads cannot supply downward regulation because they cannot consume outside their baseline. Scope: Directional availability of curtailable disconnectable loads in the allocation model. Condition: When allocating downward regulation. The scheduled consumption profile must fit the permitted shifting window. Postponement preserves the consumption profile, cannot split it, and allows only one shift per shifting period. Scope: Shiftable-profile load feasibility in local allocation. Condition: For each appliance and its permitted shifting periods, including when the profile is not postponed. The case-study allocation includes a terminal constraint restoring stored energy to its initial amount at the end of the simulation horizon. Scope: Battery terminal-energy restriction in the simulated case study.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Availability restrictions on contracted flexibility used in local allocation. | Battery feasibility constraints in local allocation. | Battery terminal-energy restriction in the simulated case study. | Configured operating bounds for curtailable disconnectable loads in local allocation. | Continuity of curtailable disconnectable load schedules in local allocation. | Daily allocation sequence described for the case study. | Directional availability of curtailable disconnectable loads in the allocation model. | Disconnectable-generator feasibility restrictions in local allocation. | Downward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem. | Local allocation priorities under the Traffic Light Concept used in the formulation. | Objective of the local flexibility allocation problem. | Reducible-generator feasibility constraints in local allocation. | SESP scheduling of local flexible resources for the operation day. | SESP-operated local flexibility market serving DSO power-quality requirements. | Service scope of the presented local flexibility market. | Shiftable-profile load feasibility in local allocation. | Upward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-004","v141-b-g01-ev-006","v141-b-g01-ev-007","v141-b-g01-ev-010","v141-b-g01-ev-013","v141-b-g02-ev05","v141-b-g02-ev10","v141-b-g02-ev11","v141-b-g02-ev13","v141-b-g02-ev15","v141-b-g02-ev20","v141-b-g02-ev21","v141-b-g02-ev22","v141-b-g02-ev23","v141-b-g02-ev25","v141-b-g02-ev29","v141-b-g02-ev33","v141-b-g02-ev34","v141-b-g02-ev36","v141-b-g02-ev37","v141-b-g03-E13","v141-b-g03-E15","v141-b-g04-e03"]},"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"13aaf9ab97f39a93298400dacbf1f3833b654760ae93a47ef02ed6c7d66f1361","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"The SESP, acting as aggregator, receives DSO flexibility requests specifying minimum active-energy variations relative to the forecast baseline, without knowing grid-status information. Scope: DSO upward- and downward-regulation requests in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. The SESP's coordination of corrective actions must give DSO needs maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads. Scope: SESP coordination of corrective actions in the local flexibility market. Condition: During a DSO-designated yellow-light grid situation. The SESP may send direct control signals overriding local household energy-management decisions. Scope: SESP control authority over local household resource decisions. Condition: During a grid-constrained yellow-light situation. The SESP's forecast must have DSO approval before it is used as the baseline scenario. Scope: Use of SESP forecasts as the flexibility-market baseline. Condition: When a consumption or generation forecast for a flexible asset is to be used as the baseline scenario.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"DSO upward- and downward-regulation requests in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. | SESP control authority over local household resource decisions. | SESP coordination of corrective actions in the local flexibility market. | Use of SESP forecasts as the flexibility-market baseline.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-003","v141-b-g01-ev-004","v141-b-g01-ev-011","v141-b-g01-ev-014","v141-b-g02-ev33"]},"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","source_rule_sha256":"f0a3f24e00e9c27d1e767402691fddd0233d7645925a90b115075bfc3ea2381f","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"The SESP market subsystem manages local flexibility transactions, including settlement, billing and accounting applications. Scope: Settlement administration within the SESP market subsystem for local flexibility transactions. Flexibility revenues are paid according to the flexibility contracts under the pay-as-contract arrangement. Scope: Flexibility-provider revenues in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. Participants with activated resources are subsequently rewarded according to their flexibility contracts. Scope: Rewards to participants whose resources are activated in response to DSO requirements. Condition: The participant's resources have been activated. The flexibility cost used to determine compensation for curtailable and shiftable loads does not depend on the amount of activated energy. Scope: Compensation for flexibility supplied by curtailable and shiftable loads. Condition: Flexibility from a curtailable or shiftable load is activated. The disconnection cost equals the number of disconnected periods multiplied by the load's disconnection fee. Scope: Contractual flexibility charges for curtailable disconnectable loads. Condition: A curtailable disconnectable load is disconnected. The shifting cost equals the consumption delay multiplied by the cost per time unit established in the load's flexibility contract. Scope: Contractual flexibility charges for shiftable profile loads. Condition: A shiftable load's consumption is delayed relative to its baseline start. Generator-curtailment fees are established in the agreed flexibility contract, with prices specified period by period for reducing or disconnecting generation. Scope: Contractual flexibility charges for reducible and disconnectable generators. Condition: Generation is reduced or disconnected.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Compensation for flexibility supplied by curtailable and shiftable loads. | Contractual flexibility charges for curtailable disconnectable loads. | Contractual flexibility charges for reducible and disconnectable generators. | Contractual flexibility charges for shiftable profile loads. | Flexibility-provider revenues in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. | Rewards to participants whose resources are activated in response to DSO requirements. | Settlement administration within the SESP market subsystem for local flexibility transactions.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-008","v141-b-g01-ev-010","v141-b-g01-ev-015","v141-b-g02-ev03","v141-b-g02-ev12","v141-b-g02-ev24","v141-b-g02-ev32"]},"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"a31774bd19f15e662feb18fabebda57985938118826af388fa240fbd03b3f902","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","market_meaning":"The utility, acting as coordinator of the community's ancillary-service procurement, determines which bids are accepted and the quantities procured. Scope: The utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community during the ancillary-service Agreement Period. Condition: After collecting customers' ancillary-service bids. Procurement quotas should initially distribute the requested service equally among submitting customers; quotas exceeding customers' bids are redistributed to customers needing additional quotas. Scope: Ancillary-service quota allocation among submitting customers in the P2P energy trading community, separately for each target time slot. Condition: After collecting ancillary-service bids, during quota distribution. Set the customer's final procurement quota equal to its submitted ancillary-service bid. Scope: Step III of ancillary-service procurement allocation, for each submitting customer and target time slot. Condition: The customer's distributed quota is at least its submitted ancillary-service bid. The customer chooses its final procurement quota between zero and its distributed quota, inclusive. Scope: Step III of ancillary-service procurement allocation, for each submitting customer and target time slot. Condition: The customer's distributed quota is below its submitted ancillary-service bid.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Ancillary-service quota allocation among submitting customers in the P2P energy trading community, separately for each target time slot. | Step III of ancillary-service procurement allocation, for each submitting customer and target time slot. | The utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community during the ancillary-service Agreement Period.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness","v141-b-g02-E12-fig3-quota-algorithm"]},"constraint_refs":["25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"12bf5d09d9e158ac17ee525b2ca0faa70662119a8c18af6e50c91abdc53dec5f","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M03-ancillary-service::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-ancillary-service","anchor":false},{"id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R015","market_meaning":"The utility, acting as the community's ancillary-service procurement coordinator, issues incentive signals by broadcasting required service quantities and offered service prices to all community customers. Scope: The power utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community for the Target Time Period T. Condition: Based on assessment of the future operational status of the power system. The published ancillary-service incentive prices are fixed by the utility. Scope: Ancillary-service incentive prices published in Step 1 and used for customer bidding in Step 2.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Ancillary-service incentive prices published in Step 1 and used for customer bidding in Step 2. | The power utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community for the Target Time Period T.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E10-as-step2-bid-form-eq17","v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness"]},"constraint_refs":["25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C001","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C003","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C004","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C005"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"35b004df0a1177c1cc79f7d929ee5b54a4335afeb51965616f307a0f74c4e283","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M03-ancillary-service::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-ancillary-service","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-001","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"be In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for or sending information, t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-010","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"re e- i- ty ed he Fig. 3. Consumer’s ﬂexibility oﬀers example. ed y- resources sorted from the cheap","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Therefore, according to the DSO requirements to manage power quality issues, the SESP decides which resources are necessary to meet the request and sends the control signals to them. After that, the participants with activated resources are then rewarded based on their flexibility contract.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Therefore, according to the DSO requirements to manage power quality issues, the SESP decides which resources are necessary to meet the request and sends the control signals to them. After that, the participants with activated resources are then rewarded based on their flexibility contract."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-001","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"be In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for or sending information, t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-003","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":2,"printed_page":"882","section":"2.1. Centralized local flexibility market approaches","anchor_text":"on same distribution transformer have to be members of the same BRP and diﬀerent BRP could not be in","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In contrast  in  this work, AGR receives DSO requests without knowing grid status information to solve grid congestion problems in the daily basis. In order to attend its demands, AGR controls flexible assets using a market-based methodology.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"In contrast  in  this work, AGR receives DSO requests without knowing grid status information to solve grid congestion problems in the daily basis. In order to attend its demands, AGR controls flexible assets using a market-based methodology."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-ev33","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p08-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"888","section":"4.5. DSO request constraints","anchor_text":", nd Tkend,c − (V kend,c − Vkstart,c ) me ρk ,c = ∑ γk ,t · t ∀ k ∈ K SP ,∀ c ∈ C (k ) t = Tkstart,c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Finally, DSO constraints represent the request for fulﬁlling upward and downward regulation. These constraints are deﬁned as the minimum required amount of active energy variation with respect to the forecasted baseline scenario and are denoted as DtDSO. Positives and negative values of DtDSO mean upward and downward regulation re-spectively.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Finally, DSO constraints represent the request for fulﬁlling upward and downward regulation. These constraints are deﬁned as the minimum required amount of active energy variation with respect to the forecasted baseline scenario and are denoted as DtDSO. Positives and negative values of DtDSO mean upward and downward regulation re-spectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-004","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"[ ] w In contrast to the previous studied works, this paper presents a te novel and innovative propo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Furthermore and according to the Traffic Light Concept (TLC) proposed by the German Association of Energy and Water (BDEW) [44] and the study by Zoeller et al. [45], the present problem formulation is designed for situations where the DSO determined a yellow light situation in a grid zone and it wants to go back to the green light status. Thereafter, the DSO asks the SESP to apply corrective actions in exchange for economic compensation previously established on their contract. During yellow light situations, the DSO needs have maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads and prosumers or ARG priorities are not considered.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Furthermore and according to the Traffic Light Concept (TLC) proposed by the German Association of Energy and Water (BDEW) [44] and the study by Zoeller et al. [45], the present problem formulation is designed for situations where the DSO determined a yellow light situation in a grid zone and it wants to go back to the green light status. Thereafter, the DSO asks the SESP to apply corrective actions in exchange for economic compensation previously established on their contract. During yellow light situations, the DSO needs have maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads and prosumers or ARG priorities are not considered."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-014","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"ge LC are small computers with communication capabilities for rehouseholds to monitor and control pr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Local controllers can take decisions during non-constrained situations about the energy management of the household resources. Nevertheless, during grid constrained situations like yellow and red light, the SESP and the DSO can send direct control signals respectively avoiding the local decisions.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Local controllers can take decisions during non-constrained situations about the energy management of the household resources. Nevertheless, during grid constrained situations like yellow and red light, the SESP and the DSO can send direct control signals respectively avoiding the local decisions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-011","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-activation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Fig. 4. System des 1. The consumption or generation forecast of every ﬂexible asset is re done by th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"1. The consumption or generation forecast of every flexible asset is done by the SESP and it needs the approval of the DSO to be used as the base line scenario.\n2. Once the SESP sends a command to a flexible resource in order to modify its generation/consumption, the amount of the activated flexibility is counted as the absolute value of the difference between the predicted power consumption/generation and the measured power in the device metering point.\n3. The activation of the flexibility ends when the SESP sends an END command permitting the resource to operate freely again.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"1. The consumption or generation forecast of every flexible asset is done by the SESP and it needs the approval of the DSO to be used as the base line scenario.\n2. Once the SESP sends a command to a flexible resource in order to modify its generation/consumption, the amount of the activated flexibility is counted as the absolute value of the difference between the predicted power consumption/generation and the measured power in the device metering point.\n3. The activation of the flexibility ends when the SESP sends an END command permitting the resource to operate freely again."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-001","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"be In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for or sending information, t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-015","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"The domains aﬀected cover from prosumers and DER installations D up to the distribution grid. The zo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The zones identified as Market and Enterprise comprise three subsystems that make SESP operation possible. They are the market, control and metering systems. The market is responsible for the management of transactions needed to implement the local flexibility market. It covers energy scheduling, flexibility, settlement, billing and accounting applications. The control subsystem is in charge of the management of the orders determined in the market. The metering subsystem manages the data resulting from smart meters (SM) and local controllers (LC) on the field zone.","paper":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The zones identified as Market and Enterprise comprise three subsystems that make SESP operation possible. They are the market, control and metering systems. The market is responsible for the management of transactions needed to implement the local flexibility market. It covers energy scheduling, flexibility, settlement, billing and accounting applications. The control subsystem is in charge of the management of the orders determined in the market. The metering subsystem manages the data resulting from smart meters (SM) and local controllers (LC) on the field zone."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper.","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","quote":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M03-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 278 (2020) 115671 ng After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy trading community (a time window can be defined in the Agreement Period for this purpose), the power utility needs to decide which of and to what extent the bids are to be accepted/declined. To achieve the fairness, the power utility in principle should distribute the amount of services to be procured averagely on the customers who have submitted the bids. However, the amount of services to be procured from each customer (called as the 'quota') calculated in this way may be higher than some customers' bids, and thus the surplus quotas can be given to other customers in need. With the above considerations, the process detailed in Fig. 3 is proposed for the power utility to decide the amount of services to be procured from the customers.","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","quote":"After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy trading community (a time window can be defined in the Agreement Period for this purpose), the power utility needs to decide which of and to what extent the bids are to be accepted/declined. To achieve the fairness, the power utility in principle should distribute the amount of services to be procured averagely on the customers who have submitted the bids. However, the amount of services to be procured from each customer (called as the 'quota') calculated in this way may be higher than some customers' bids, and thus the surplus quotas can be given to other customers in need. With the above considerations, the process detailed in Fig. 3 is proposed for the power utility to decide the amount of services to be procured from the customers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M03-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"2. Methodological framework ri in In this paper, a framework for ancillary service provision from a ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"After that, in the ancillary service mechanism (shown as the red dashed circle) the power utility will assess its operational needs and issue incentive signals for obtaining ancillary services, and the customers will respond to the signals by submitting bids for providing ancillary services based on the consideration of maximizing their respective economic benefits. The power utility will then assess the bids and make purchase decisions.","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_id":"v141-c-R015","quote":"After that, in the ancillary service mechanism (shown as the red dashed circle) the power utility will assess its operational needs and issue incentive signals for obtaining ancillary services, and the customers will respond to the signals by submitting bids for providing ancillary services based on the consideration of maximizing their respective economic benefits. The power utility will then assess the bids and make purchase decisions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper.","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_id":"v141-c-R015","quote":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M03-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 278 (2020) 115671 ng After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy trading community (a time window can be defined in the Agreement Period for this purpose), the power utility needs to decide which of and to what extent the bids are to be accepted/declined. To achieve the fairness, the power utility in principle should distribute the amount of services to be procured averagely on the customers who have submitted the bids. However, the amount of services to be procured from each customer (called as the 'quota') calculated in this way may be higher than some customers' bids, and thus the surplus quotas can be given to other customers in need. With the above considerations, the process detailed in Fig. 3 is proposed for the power utility to decide the amount of services to be procured from the customers.","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","rule_id":"v141-c-R015","quote":"After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy trading community (a time window can be defined in the Agreement Period for this purpose), the power utility needs to decide which of and to what extent the bids are to be accepted/declined. To achieve the fairness, the power utility in principle should distribute the amount of services to be procured averagely on the customers who have submitted the bids. However, the amount of services to be procured from each customer (called as the 'quota') calculated in this way may be higher than some customers' bids, and thus the surplus quotas can be given to other customers in need. With the above considerations, the process detailed in Fig. 3 is proposed for the power utility to decide the amount of services to be procured from the customers."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["service_market_operator_authority","service_procurement_organizer"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L4-5703bbe65e","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L4","ontological_kind":"service_market_operator_authority"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"A coordinating entity that, in response to power system operation service demand, organizes community service supply and holds the decision-making authority to select supply resources.","object":"The organizational responsibilities and supply selection authority among the system service demand side, community suppliers, and the coordinating entity.","inputs":"System operation service demand and community candidate service supply information.","operation":"Based on the service demand, the coordinating entity decides which community supplies to adopt within the supply conditions and participant authority specified by the source.","outputs":"The selection decision for community service supply and the attribution of coordination responsibility.","boundary":"Limited to organizational responsibilities and resource selection authority within L4 and SRP. Whether the demand side and the coordinator are undertaken by the same entity, direct control rights, baseline approval, and procurement procedures are all retained as membership conditions; clearing algorithms, price formation, or payment formulas are not merged, nor is the coordinator required to have unrestricted unilateral control."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","paper_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.local_operator","source_occurrences":[{"id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The DSO's requested flexibility supplies the input for determining local resource allocations over the operation day. Scope: SESP scheduling of local flexible resources for the operation day. The requested upward active-energy variation imposes a minimum on the net upward regulation allocated relative to the forecasted baseline; contributions opposing the request remain included in the constraint. Scope: Upward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem. Condition: During upward-regulation periods with a positive DSO request. The requested downward active-energy variation imposes a minimum on the magnitude of net downward regulation allocated relative to the forecasted baseline; contributions opposing the request remain included in the constraint. Scope: Downward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem. Condition: During downward-regulation periods with a negative DSO request. The DSO sends its flexibility needs before the SESP executes the daily local allocation algorithm. Scope: Daily allocation sequence described for the case study. The SESP, acting as the local market coordinator, determines which flexible resources are allocated to meet the DSO request. Scope: SESP-operated local flexibility market serving DSO power-quality requirements. Local allocation minimizes the SESP's operating cost of meeting the DSO request over the following periods, using flexibility costs predefined in SESP-community member contracts. Scope: Objective of the local flexibility allocation problem. Local flexibility allocation is restricted to providing flexibility to the DSO. Scope: Service scope of the presented local flexibility market. DSO needs receive maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads; prosumer or aggregator priorities are not considered. Scope: Local allocation priorities under the Traffic Light Concept used in the formulation. Condition: During yellow light situations. Allocation must respect the contracted times during which the offered flexibility can be used. Scope: Availability restrictions on contracted flexibility used in local allocation. Condition: When allocating resources whose flexibility contracts specify permitted usage times. Allocated generation curtailment is nonnegative and cannot exceed the forecasted generation. Scope: Reducible-generator feasibility constraints in local allocation. Condition: For reducible generators in each scheduling period. Allocation permits only switching these generators on or off; intermediate production setpoints are unavailable. Scope: Disconnectable-generator feasibility restrictions in local allocation. Condition: For disconnectable generators. Battery allocations must respect per-period charging and discharging limits and maximum storage capacity, with charging and discharging efficiencies included in state-of-charge accounting. Scope: Battery feasibility constraints in local allocation. Condition: When allocating battery charging or discharging. The allocation schedule must either maintain the disconnection or reconnect the load in the current period. Scope: Continuity of curtailable disconnectable load schedules in local allocation. Condition: After a load was disconnected or remained disconnected in the preceding period. Allocation schedules must respect the maximum number of disconnection orders per day, the minimum resting time between disconnections and the maximum disconnection duration, as applicable. Scope: Configured operating bounds for curtailable disconnectable loads in local allocation. Condition: When the corresponding disconnection-count, resting-time or duration bounds are specified. Curtailable disconnectable loads cannot supply downward regulation because they cannot consume outside their baseline. Scope: Directional availability of curtailable disconnectable loads in the allocation model. Condition: When allocating downward regulation. The scheduled consumption profile must fit the permitted shifting window. Postponement preserves the consumption profile, cannot split it, and allows only one shift per shifting period. Scope: Shiftable-profile load feasibility in local allocation. Condition: For each appliance and its permitted shifting periods, including when the profile is not postponed. The case-study allocation includes a terminal constraint restoring stored energy to its initial amount at the end of the simulation horizon. Scope: Battery terminal-energy restriction in the simulated case study.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Availability restrictions on contracted flexibility used in local allocation. | Battery feasibility constraints in local allocation. | Battery terminal-energy restriction in the simulated case study. | Configured operating bounds for curtailable disconnectable loads in local allocation. | Continuity of curtailable disconnectable load schedules in local allocation. | Daily allocation sequence described for the case study. | Directional availability of curtailable disconnectable loads in the allocation model. | Disconnectable-generator feasibility restrictions in local allocation. | Downward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem. | Local allocation priorities under the Traffic Light Concept used in the formulation. | Objective of the local flexibility allocation problem. | Reducible-generator feasibility constraints in local allocation. | SESP scheduling of local flexible resources for the operation day. | SESP-operated local flexibility market serving DSO power-quality requirements. | Service scope of the presented local flexibility market. | Shiftable-profile load feasibility in local allocation. | Upward-regulation constraints in the local flexibility allocation problem.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-004","v141-b-g01-ev-006","v141-b-g01-ev-007","v141-b-g01-ev-010","v141-b-g01-ev-013","v141-b-g02-ev05","v141-b-g02-ev10","v141-b-g02-ev11","v141-b-g02-ev13","v141-b-g02-ev15","v141-b-g02-ev20","v141-b-g02-ev21","v141-b-g02-ev22","v141-b-g02-ev23","v141-b-g02-ev25","v141-b-g02-ev29","v141-b-g02-ev33","v141-b-g02-ev34","v141-b-g02-ev36","v141-b-g02-ev37","v141-b-g03-E13","v141-b-g03-E15","v141-b-g04-e03"]},"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"13aaf9ab97f39a93298400dacbf1f3833b654760ae93a47ef02ed6c7d66f1361"},{"id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"The SESP, acting as aggregator, receives DSO flexibility requests specifying minimum active-energy variations relative to the forecast baseline, without knowing grid-status information. Scope: DSO upward- and downward-regulation requests in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. The SESP's coordination of corrective actions must give DSO needs maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads. Scope: SESP coordination of corrective actions in the local flexibility market. Condition: During a DSO-designated yellow-light grid situation. The SESP may send direct control signals overriding local household energy-management decisions. Scope: SESP control authority over local household resource decisions. Condition: During a grid-constrained yellow-light situation. The SESP's forecast must have DSO approval before it is used as the baseline scenario. Scope: Use of SESP forecasts as the flexibility-market baseline. Condition: When a consumption or generation forecast for a flexible asset is to be used as the baseline scenario.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"DSO upward- and downward-regulation requests in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. | SESP control authority over local household resource decisions. | SESP coordination of corrective actions in the local flexibility market. | Use of SESP forecasts as the flexibility-market baseline.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-003","v141-b-g01-ev-004","v141-b-g01-ev-011","v141-b-g01-ev-014","v141-b-g02-ev33"]},"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","source_rule_sha256":"f0a3f24e00e9c27d1e767402691fddd0233d7645925a90b115075bfc3ea2381f"},{"id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"The SESP market subsystem manages local flexibility transactions, including settlement, billing and accounting applications. Scope: Settlement administration within the SESP market subsystem for local flexibility transactions. Flexibility revenues are paid according to the flexibility contracts under the pay-as-contract arrangement. Scope: Flexibility-provider revenues in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. Participants with activated resources are subsequently rewarded according to their flexibility contracts. Scope: Rewards to participants whose resources are activated in response to DSO requirements. Condition: The participant's resources have been activated. The flexibility cost used to determine compensation for curtailable and shiftable loads does not depend on the amount of activated energy. Scope: Compensation for flexibility supplied by curtailable and shiftable loads. Condition: Flexibility from a curtailable or shiftable load is activated. The disconnection cost equals the number of disconnected periods multiplied by the load's disconnection fee. Scope: Contractual flexibility charges for curtailable disconnectable loads. Condition: A curtailable disconnectable load is disconnected. The shifting cost equals the consumption delay multiplied by the cost per time unit established in the load's flexibility contract. Scope: Contractual flexibility charges for shiftable profile loads. Condition: A shiftable load's consumption is delayed relative to its baseline start. Generator-curtailment fees are established in the agreed flexibility contract, with prices specified period by period for reducing or disconnecting generation. Scope: Contractual flexibility charges for reducible and disconnectable generators. Condition: Generation is reduced or disconnected.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Compensation for flexibility supplied by curtailable and shiftable loads. | Contractual flexibility charges for curtailable disconnectable loads. | Contractual flexibility charges for reducible and disconnectable generators. | Contractual flexibility charges for shiftable profile loads. | Flexibility-provider revenues in the SESP-operated local flexibility market. | Rewards to participants whose resources are activated in response to DSO requirements. | Settlement administration within the SESP market subsystem for local flexibility transactions.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-008","v141-b-g01-ev-010","v141-b-g01-ev-015","v141-b-g02-ev03","v141-b-g02-ev12","v141-b-g02-ev24","v141-b-g02-ev32"]},"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"a31774bd19f15e662feb18fabebda57985938118826af388fa240fbd03b3f902"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The SESP, acting as the local market coordinator, determines which flexible resources are allocated to meet the DSO request. Scope: SESP-operated local flexibility market serving DSO power-quality requirements.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-010"],"ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-001","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"be In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for or sending information, t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-010","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"re e- i- ty ed he Fig. 3. Consumer’s ﬂexibility oﬀers example. ed y- resources sorted from the cheap","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Therefore, according to the DSO requirements to manage power quality issues, the SESP decides which resources are necessary to meet the request and sends the control signals to them. After that, the participants with activated resources are then rewarded based on their flexibility contract."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The SESP, acting as aggregator, receives DSO flexibility requests specifying minimum active-energy variations relative to the forecast baseline, without knowing grid-status information. Scope: DSO upward- and downward-regulation requests in the SESP-operated local flexibility market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-003","v141-b-g02-ev33"],"ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-001","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"be In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for or sending information, t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-003","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":2,"printed_page":"882","section":"2.1. Centralized local flexibility market approaches","anchor_text":"on same distribution transformer have to be members of the same BRP and diﬀerent BRP could not be in","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In contrast  in  this work, AGR receives DSO requests without knowing grid status information to solve grid congestion problems in the daily basis. In order to attend its demands, AGR controls flexible assets using a market-based methodology."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-ev33","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p08-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":"888","section":"4.5. DSO request constraints","anchor_text":", nd Tkend,c − (V kend,c − Vkstart,c ) me ρk ,c = ∑ γk ,t · t ∀ k ∈ K SP ,∀ c ∈ C (k ) t = Tkstart,c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Finally, DSO constraints represent the request for fulﬁlling upward and downward regulation. These constraints are deﬁned as the minimum required amount of active energy variation with respect to the forecasted baseline scenario and are denoted as DtDSO. Positives and negative values of DtDSO mean upward and downward regulation re-spectively."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The SESP's coordination of corrective actions must give DSO needs maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads. Scope: SESP coordination of corrective actions in the local flexibility market. Condition: During a DSO-designated yellow-light grid situation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-004"],"ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-004","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"[ ] w In contrast to the previous studied works, this paper presents a te novel and innovative propo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Furthermore and according to the Traffic Light Concept (TLC) proposed by the German Association of Energy and Water (BDEW) [44] and the study by Zoeller et al. [45], the present problem formulation is designed for situations where the DSO determined a yellow light situation in a grid zone and it wants to go back to the green light status. Thereafter, the DSO asks the SESP to apply corrective actions in exchange for economic compensation previously established on their contract. During yellow light situations, the DSO needs have maximum priority to avoid over-voltages or transformer over-loads and prosumers or ARG priorities are not considered."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The SESP may send direct control signals overriding local household energy-management decisions. Scope: SESP control authority over local household resource decisions. Condition: During a grid-constrained yellow-light situation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-014"],"ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-014","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"ge LC are small computers with communication capabilities for rehouseholds to monitor and control pr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Local controllers can take decisions during non-constrained situations about the energy management of the household resources. Nevertheless, during grid constrained situations like yellow and red light, the SESP and the DSO can send direct control signals respectively avoiding the local decisions."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The SESP's forecast must have DSO approval before it is used as the baseline scenario. Scope: Use of SESP forecasts as the flexibility-market baseline. Condition: When a consumption or generation forecast for a flexible asset is to be used as the baseline scenario.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-011"],"ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-011","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-activation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Fig. 4. System des 1. The consumption or generation forecast of every ﬂexible asset is re done by th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"1. The consumption or generation forecast of every flexible asset is done by the SESP and it needs the approval of the DSO to be used as the base line scenario.\n2. Once the SESP sends a command to a flexible resource in order to modify its generation/consumption, the amount of the activated flexibility is counted as the absolute value of the difference between the predicted power consumption/generation and the measured power in the device metering point.\n3. The activation of the flexibility ends when the SESP sends an END command permitting the resource to operate freely again."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The SESP market subsystem manages local flexibility transactions, including settlement, billing and accounting applications. Scope: Settlement administration within the SESP market subsystem for local flexibility transactions.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-001","v141-b-g01-ev-015"],"ref":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-001","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"be In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for or sending information, t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A local flexibility market (LFM) is an electricity trading platform to sell and buy flexibility in geographically limited areas like neighbourhoods and small towns. The SESP is the local market platform provider and community aggregator (AGR). At the same time, the SESP is a BRP from the regulatory point of view because it bids in wholesale markets. In order to run these markets, local traders need the SESP Platform for sending information, trading for flexibility, and scheduling actions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-015","location":{"location_id":"src-58f2111926-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"The domains aﬀected cover from prosumers and DER installations D up to the distribution grid. The zo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The zones identified as Market and Enterprise comprise three subsystems that make SESP operation possible. They are the market, control and metering systems. The market is responsible for the management of transactions needed to implement the local flexibility market. It covers energy scheduling, flexibility, settlement, billing and accounting applications. The control subsystem is in charge of the management of the orders determined in the market. The metering subsystem manages the data resulting from smart meters (SM) and local controllers (LC) on the field zone."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[]},{"unit_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M03-ancillary-service::coordination.local_operator","paper_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-ancillary-service","function":"coordination.local_operator","source_occurrences":[{"id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","market_meaning":"The utility, acting as coordinator of the community's ancillary-service procurement, determines which bids are accepted and the quantities procured. Scope: The utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community during the ancillary-service Agreement Period. Condition: After collecting customers' ancillary-service bids. Procurement quotas should initially distribute the requested service equally among submitting customers; quotas exceeding customers' bids are redistributed to customers needing additional quotas. Scope: Ancillary-service quota allocation among submitting customers in the P2P energy trading community, separately for each target time slot. Condition: After collecting ancillary-service bids, during quota distribution. Set the customer's final procurement quota equal to its submitted ancillary-service bid. Scope: Step III of ancillary-service procurement allocation, for each submitting customer and target time slot. Condition: The customer's distributed quota is at least its submitted ancillary-service bid. The customer chooses its final procurement quota between zero and its distributed quota, inclusive. Scope: Step III of ancillary-service procurement allocation, for each submitting customer and target time slot. Condition: The customer's distributed quota is below its submitted ancillary-service bid.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Ancillary-service quota allocation among submitting customers in the P2P energy trading community, separately for each target time slot. | Step III of ancillary-service procurement allocation, for each submitting customer and target time slot. | The utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community during the ancillary-service Agreement Period.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness","v141-b-g02-E12-fig3-quota-algorithm"]},"constraint_refs":["25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"12bf5d09d9e158ac17ee525b2ca0faa70662119a8c18af6e50c91abdc53dec5f"},{"id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R015","market_meaning":"The utility, acting as the community's ancillary-service procurement coordinator, issues incentive signals by broadcasting required service quantities and offered service prices to all community customers. Scope: The power utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community for the Target Time Period T. Condition: Based on assessment of the future operational status of the power system. The published ancillary-service incentive prices are fixed by the utility. Scope: Ancillary-service incentive prices published in Step 1 and used for customer bidding in Step 2.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Ancillary-service incentive prices published in Step 1 and used for customer bidding in Step 2. | The power utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community for the Target Time Period T.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E10-as-step2-bid-form-eq17","v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness"]},"constraint_refs":["25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C001","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C003","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C004","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C005"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"35b004df0a1177c1cc79f7d929ee5b54a4335afeb51965616f307a0f74c4e283"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R014-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The utility, acting as coordinator of the community's ancillary-service procurement, determines which bids are accepted and the quantities procured. Scope: The utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community during the ancillary-service Agreement Period. Condition: After collecting customers' ancillary-service bids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness"],"ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M03-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 278 (2020) 115671 ng After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy trading community (a time window can be defined in the Agreement Period for this purpose), the power utility needs to decide which of and to what extent the bids are to be accepted/declined. To achieve the fairness, the power utility in principle should distribute the amount of services to be procured averagely on the customers who have submitted the bids. However, the amount of services to be procured from each customer (called as the 'quota') calculated in this way may be higher than some customers' bids, and thus the surplus quotas can be given to other customers in need. With the above considerations, the process detailed in Fig. 3 is proposed for the power utility to decide the amount of services to be procured from the customers."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R015-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The utility, acting as the community's ancillary-service procurement coordinator, issues incentive signals by broadcasting required service quantities and offered service prices to all community customers. Scope: The power utility's coordination of ancillary-service procurement from customers in the P2P energy trading community for the Target Time Period T. Condition: Based on assessment of the future operational status of the power system.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta-DAS","theta-pAS"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness"],"ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R015","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M03-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"2. Methodological framework ri in In this paper, a framework for ancillary service provision from a ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"After that, in the ancillary service mechanism (shown as the red dashed circle) the power utility will assess its operational needs and issue incentive signals for obtaining ancillary services, and the customers will respond to the signals by submitting bids for providing ancillary services based on the consideration of maximizing their respective economic benefits. The power utility will then assess the bids and make purchase decisions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11-as-step3-fairness","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M03-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 278 (2020) 115671 ng After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"After collecting the bids from the customers in the P2P energy trading community (a time window can be defined in the Agreement Period for this purpose), the power utility needs to decide which of and to what extent the bids are to be accepted/declined. To achieve the fairness, the power utility in principle should distribute the amount of services to be procured averagely on the customers who have submitted the bids. However, the amount of services to be procured from each customer (called as the 'quota') calculated in this way may be higher than some customers' bids, and thus the surplus quotas can be given to other customers in need. With the above considerations, the process detailed in Fig. 3 is proposed for the power utility to decide the amount of services to be procured from the customers."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R015-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The published ancillary-service total-demand and price schedules are defined for the Target Time Period T by D^{AS} = {D_t^{AS} | t ∈ T} (15) and p^{AS} = {p_t^{AS} | t ∈ T} (16), where D_t^{AS} is the required service magnitude for slot t and p_t^{AS} is the offered service price for slot t.","expression":"D^{AS} = \\{D_t^{AS} \\mid t \\in T\\}, \\quad p^{AS} = \\{p_t^{AS} \\mid t \\in T\\}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta-DAS","theta-pAS"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication"],"ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R015","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R015-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"supporting","statement":"The power utility specifies the type of ancillary services needed, either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication"],"ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R015","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R015-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"exception","statement":"How the power utility decides D^{AS} and p^{AS} involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility and is out of the scope of this paper; no utility-side optimization for these signals is specified.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta-DAS","theta-pAS"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication"],"ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R015","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"of the net load of a customer as a whole, rather than the change of power output/consumption of spec","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the first step, based on the assessment of the future operational status of power systems, the power utility will broadcast the total demand and prices of ancillary services to all the customers in the P2P energy trading community: \\(D^{AS}=\\{D_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (15) \\(p^{AS}=\\{p_t^{AS}\\,|\\,t\\in T\\}\\), (16) where \\(D^{AS}\\) is the set of the magnitude (kW) of the ancillary services required for the time slots within the Target Time Period T, and \\(p^{AS}\\) is the set of prices (£/kW/h) that the power utility is willing to pay for the ancillary services provided by the customers. The power utility will also specify the type of ancillary services needed (either 'Type-1 AS' or 'Type-2 AS'). Note that how the power utility decides \\(D^{AS}\\) and \\(p^{AS}\\) involves comprehensive analysis of the power systems managed by the power utility, which is out of the scope of this paper."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta-DAS","symbol":"D_t^{AS}","meaning":"Magnitude (kW) of ancillary service required for time slot t, published by the power utility as the set D^{AS} over the Target Time Period T.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["t ∈ T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R015","declaration_ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::theta::theta-DAS"},{"parameter_id":"theta-pAS","symbol":"p_t^{AS}","meaning":"Ancillary-service incentive price (£/kW/h) for time slot t that the power utility is willing to pay, published as the set p^{AS} over the Target Time Period T and fixed for customer bidding.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"£/kW/h","indices":["t ∈ T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E09-as-step1-publication","v141-b-g02-E10-as-step2-bid-form-eq17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R015","declaration_ref":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::theta::theta-pAS"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","stage":"L4","subtype":"service_market_operator_authority","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"SESP serves as the local platform provider and community aggregation coordinator, receives DSO demand, and decides on resource dispatch; its forecast baseline must be approved by the DSO. In the yellow-light state, DSO demand has priority, and SESP has the authority to override household decisions through direct control; the SESP market subsystem bears transaction and settlement management responsibilities.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: the organizational relationship among the service requester, market operator, and flexibility supplier","Core relationship: the operator receives the request and obtains resource selection and coordination control authority","Output: the allocation of responsibilities and authority for resource dispatch, baseline approval, and transaction management"],"variation_fields":["The operator and community aggregator are SESP, and the service requester is the DSO","SESP does not possess grid state information","The DSO approves the forecast baseline generated by SESP","The yellow-light state triggers priority for DSO demand and SESP's authority to override local decisions","The SESP market subsystem is responsible for settlement, billing, and accounting management"],"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"paper_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.local_operator","template_code":"SRP"},{"unit_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M03-ancillary-service::coordination.local_operator","stage":"L4","subtype":"service_procurement_organizer","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The utility serves as the coordinator of ancillary service procurement, publishes information on required services and remuneration, collects customer declarations, and is responsible for deciding which declarations to accept and the extent of procurement.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: organizer of ancillary service procurement","Core responsibility: publish procurement information, collect declarations, and make procurement decisions","Output: the procurement coordination process for which the utility is responsible"],"variation_fields":["Publish information according to future power system operation needs","The service type is Type-1 AS or Type-2 AS","A declaration collection window can be set within the Agreement Period","The utility-side method for deriving demand and prices is outside the scope of the paper"],"constraint_refs":["25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C001","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C004","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C005"],"paper_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-ancillary-service","function":"coordination.local_operator","template_code":"SRP"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","source_definition":"SESP serves as the local platform provider and community aggregation coordinator, receives DSO demand, and decides on resource dispatch; its forecast baseline must be approved by the DSO. In the yellow-light state, DSO demand has priority, and SESP has the authority to override household decisions through direct control; the SESP market subsystem bears transaction and settlement management responsibilities.","retained_variants":["SESP also serves as the local platform provider and community aggregator, and does not possess grid state information when receiving DSO requests.","The request is the minimum active energy change relative to the forecast baseline; positive and negative values indicate upward and downward regulation, respectively; it is not equated with another member's kW demand value.","SESP generates a forecast of flexible assets, which must be approved by the DSO before it can be used as a baseline.","In the yellow-light state, DSO demand has the highest priority, and SESP may override household decisions; the source text attributes direct control under the red light to the DSO, and this cannot be expanded into a general authority of SESP.","The SESP market subsystem is responsible for transaction, settlement, billing, and accounting management; these are organizational responsibilities, not common payment rules."],"constraint_refs":["21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"]},{"unit_id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M03-ancillary-service::coordination.local_operator","source_definition":"The utility serves as the coordinator of ancillary service procurement, publishes information on required services and remuneration, collects customer declarations, and is responsible for deciding which declarations to accept and the extent of procurement.","retained_variants":["The utility organizes procurement according to future system operation needs and designates Type-1 AS or Type-2 AS.","Publish D^{AS}={D_t^{AS}|t∈T} and p^{AS}={p_t^{AS}|t∈T}, with units of kW and £/kW/h, respectively; the price is fixed when the customer declares.","Within the Agreement Period, a declaration collection window can be set, and a procurement decision is made after collection.","Procurement quotas are in principle equally divided, and any surplus beyond customer declarations is reallocated; the given source context also retains the customer's authority, when the quota is insufficient, to choose the final quantity between zero and the allocated quota.","The utility-side method for deriving demand and prices is outside the scope of the paper, and common optimization or pricing rules cannot be fabricated."],"constraint_refs":["25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C001","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C003","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C004","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R015::v141-c-R015-C005"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"A coordinating entity that, in response to power system operation service demand, organizes community service supply and holds the decision-making authority to select supply resources.","object":"The organizational responsibilities and supply selection authority among the system service demand side, community suppliers, and the coordinating entity.","inputs":"System operation service demand and community candidate service supply information.","operation":"Based on the service demand, the coordinating entity decides which community supplies to adopt within the supply conditions and participant authority specified by the source.","outputs":"The selection decision for community service supply and the attribution of coordination responsibility.","boundary":"Limited to organizational responsibilities and resource selection authority within L4 and SRP. Whether the demand side and the coordinator are undertaken by the same entity, direct control rights, baseline approval, and procurement procedures are all retained as membership conditions; clearing algorithms, price formation, or payment formulas are not merged, nor is the coordinator required to have unrestricted unilateral control."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/83"});
